# He Sought a Lawyer
Tamil title: தேடியது வக்கீலை

"A terrible blow! Blood is pouring like a flood, Doctor! Please come quickly. The man has fainted."

"Why, sir, who was struck?"

"This man."

"No. From the noise you make, I thought it was you who had been struck and were screaming. You have no sense of propriety. You may shout or crow anywhere else, but there must be silence where doctors work. Understand?"

"All right. Sorry!"

The doctor leisurely gave the nurse some instructions - medical instructions, of course - and then came over. The injured man lay upon a bench, his whole shirt soaked in blood. Beside him stood the man who had hurried the doctor.

"What is this?" the doctor asked. His voice, manner, and look resembled those of a police officer.

"He is my friend. He was caught in a terrible situation and they beat him like this."

"Should you not go to the police? Report how he was struck, the name of the assailant, and the place; they will then summon me."

"We cannot go to the police."

"Why? Did he get drunk and create a scene? Was he beaten at a courtesan's house? Or thrashed while stealing? What mystery made him suffer a beating the police must not know about?"

"Good heavens! You suspect all sorts of needless things. It was nothing like that. His own relatives beat him. I merely meant there was no need to report it to the police. Nothing more."

During this conversation, in accordance with the doctor's plan, the compounder cleaned the wound, applied medicine, and bandaged it. The injured man's eyes opened a little and closed again. The doctor ordered a mouthful of milk to be poured into him. That ceremony too ended, and the compounder began preparing the bill.

"Name?"

"My name?"

"No, man. His name."

"His name is Irudayanathan."

"Address?"

"For the present, my own address: Number 18, Pakkiri Pillai Lane."

"Your name?"

"Emperuman Pillai."

The bill was made and paid. Emperuman Pillai then respectfully raised another matter with the doctor.

"He must remain here tonight. There is no convenience elsewhere. A wedding party has arrived at my house."

The doctor ordered another bill to be prepared, instructed the compounder to attend to the patient, got into his car, and went to his bungalow. He asked no further questions about why or how the man had been beaten, nor did he say whether the injury was serious or slight. He had seen countless cases of this sort. Would this one shock or surprise him?

Irudayanathan began tossing upon the bed and babbling. Emperuman gently asked, "What is it, Irudayam? Does it hurt badly? Do you want milk? Fruit?" Irudayanathan did not answer. He uttered disconnected words and stared wildly. The compounder merely repeated his familiar lesson: everything would be all right in the morning.

At midnight Irudayanathan babbled with some alarm. Emperuman ran to him and asked affectionately, "What is it, Irudayam?" Irudayanathan could not even recognise who was speaking.

"Where am I now?" he asked.

"At the doctor's house, my boy."

"The doctor's house? Is this not a lawyer's house?"

Only then did the compounder become a little afraid.

"Why, sir, is this man mad?" he asked Emperuman.

"No, sir. Perhaps the shock of the blow affected his brain..."

"It may be so. There are such cases," said the compounder.

"You will never have heard of a case like this, sir. No lawyer will have heard one either. If only you win this case for me, the whole world will praise you!" Irudayanathan cried loudly.

"What is this, sir? He keeps raving of lawyers and cases," said the compounder.

Emperuman calmed Irudayanathan and made him lie down. Then he told the compounder, "He went searching for a lawyer and bought this trouble for himself. What he sought was a lawyer; what he reached was a doctor's house."

The compounder's suspicion grew. Now this man too was talking strangely. Emperuman subtly understood his fear and smiled.

"Compounder, I am not mad, and neither is my friend. I spoke only of what actually happened. From this morning onward my friend wandered in search of a lawyer, and I went to help him. The reward for his search was a blow to the head and unconsciousness. I shall tell you the story. Listen."

One evening Emperuman went to the office of Sokkalingam Chettiar, owner of the diamond company where he worked, to ask about an important business matter. Hearing voices inside Chettiar's room, he waited outside. Chettiar was speaking furiously.

"Can you do it or not?"

"I absolutely cannot."

"Definitely not?"

"Who are you, fellow? You are a complete fool! I would sooner seize my daughter and throw her into a ruined well than give her to you."

Chettiar angrily refused to give his daughter. Emperuman was stunned, because Chettiar had no daughter.

"That is surprising," said the compounder.

"Never in my life had I known such surprise," Emperuman continued. "Chettiar had only one son and no daughter. He had no liaison with a courtesan either. He was an extremely orthodox and devout man. Imagine, then, how I felt hearing him tell someone, 'I can never give you my daughter.'

"I heard the door open and quickly hid to one side. Irudayanathan came out of the room. That was the first time I saw him. He did not see me and departed angrily. After a little while I spoke to Chettiar and went home as usual. Irudayanathan was sitting in my inner veranda!"

"Ah! The surprise grows," said the compounder.

"It had not ended. Listen. I fired questions at him: Who are you? What is your name? Why have you come? What town are you from? I did not reveal that I knew of his conversation with Chettiar.

"'Your name is Emperuman Pillai, is it not?' he asked.

"'Yes.'

"'You keep the accounts at diamond merchant Sokkalingam Chettiar's shop?'

"'Yes.'

"'I need your help with something.'

"'I shall help if I can. First tell me who you are and what the matter is.'

"'My name is Irudayanathan. I come from a village near Tirunelveli.'

"'Very well. What do you want me to do?' Before he could answer, I asked another question. 'Were you speaking very angrily to Chettiar today?' I explained that Chettiar had said nothing to me and that I had overheard it accidentally, thereby calming his surprise.

"Irudayanathan warned me not to be startled by what he was about to say.

"'Never mind the whole story,' I said. 'First tell me one thing. Where did Chettiar get a daughter?'

"Irudayanathan laughed with open anger and disgust.

"'Sokkalingam Chettiar has no daughter; he has only one son,' he said.

"'You asked him for a bride, and he said he would sooner throw his daughter into a well than give her to you.'

"'I did not ask Sokkalingam Chettiar. I asked Swami Soroopananda.'

"I stared without understanding.

"'Naturally you do not understand. Your Sokkalingam Chettiar is our Swami Soroopananda. Just as Sokkalingam Chettiar has a son, Swami Soroopananda has a daughter. Her name is Karuna. Your surprise must have increased, but I shall quickly dispel it.

"The Sokkalingam Chettiar who trades in diamonds here roamed our region many years ago as Swami Soroopananda, conducting a business in orthodoxy. Even now you can see Soroopananda Swami's monastery there. Everyone worshipped him as a man blessed with grace. He came only once a year and stayed for one or two months. Offerings accumulated; elaborate worship took place; he preached harmony among all religions and then departed. Nobody knew where he came from, where he stayed, or where he went during the months he was absent. Everyone said he lived in the wilderness for ten months of the year, and they still say it. They called him a performer of miracles.

"They do not know the miracle: for ten months their Swami trades diamonds here as your Sokkalingam Chettiar and spends the remaining months among them as Soroopananda.

"Among those who came believing his sight would bring blessings was a widow named Jothi. Jothi was the name she took after embracing Christianity; her original name was Muthayi. Soroopananda relieved her of her widowhood, and Karuna was born. The villagers knew none of it. Jothi herself did not know that Soroopananda lived in this part of the country as Sokkalingam Chettiar.

"Jothi died while Karuna was still a child. Karuna is now eighteen, beautiful, intelligent, and educated. She sings sweetly and loves me with all her heart. She is the sovereign of my heart, and I am her Irudayanathan.'

"'How did you learn so many secrets?' I asked.

"'That too is an amusing story. A man from our village helped Sokkalingam Chettiar roam in the guise of Soroopananda, deceive the people, and take their wealth. He was the chief disciple through whom Soroopananda's fame spread.

"The townspeople would ask him, 'When will the Swami come?'

"'He appeared in my dream last night and said he would come in ten days,' the fraud would reply. His original name was Sadaiyan, but he changed it to Swami Jatadharan.

"A devotee had only to begin, 'Swami, there is a matter troubling my heart...' and Soroopananda would smile and ask, 'My child, you have bought a new house and worry whether it is auspicious, do you not?' The devotee would enter ecstasy because the Swami had read his heart. Soroopananda could reveal what lay in every devotee's mind. Why not, when letters regularly passed between Sadaiyan and Sokkalingam Chettiar?

"Eventually Sadaiyan tired of remaining Soroopananda's disciple. He told Sokkalingam not to return in that guise and announced that the Swami had merged into the Divine Light. Chettiar could not defy him. It was Sadaiyan who told me your Chettiar's history. I know it, but the townspeople still do not.

"Even if I tell them, I have no evidence. Jatadharan told me that Soroopananda had the name 'Jothi' tattooed upon his hand. By chance I saw Chettiar's hand, and it bears the tattoo 'Jega Jothi.' They advertise that as the name of one of his diamonds. Thus even the tattoo cannot help prove my claim.'

"I asked question after question. Where was Karuna? If she loved him, why did he seek Chettiar's consent? Did Karuna know Chettiar was her father?

"'Karuna was born to Jothi of our village and Soroopananda, but do you know what the village believes? Today she is the only daughter of Ellappa Mudaliar. Ellappa had long been childless. Hearing of Soroopananda's greatness, he came to our village, prayed, and conducted worship for the gift of a child. Jothi gave birth at that very time. Soroopananda called the infant a gift from God and gave her to Ellappa Mudaliar.

"'Karuna is now Ellappa's daughter. She studied with me in college. We fell in love, and I went to ask Ellappa for her hand. Karuna too courageously declared her love. Ellappa said he would consult the Guru. When I returned after some days, he firmly refused.

"'Though Ellappa could no longer ask Soroopananda directly about the child supposedly obtained through his grace, he believed he must at least secure the consent of Jatadharan, who had inherited the spiritual seat. He did not know that Swami Soroopananda was merely a fraud enacted by Sokkalingam Chettiar. Jatadharan, who knew everything about Karuna's birth and upbringing, secretly consulted Chettiar before giving his answer. Chettiar sent instructions that Jatadharan must under no circumstances permit Karuna to marry Irudayanathan.

"'Because Jatadharan withheld his command, Ellappa refused the marriage. Karuna told me that this was the hidden reason. That was all she knew. She did not know that Sokkalingam had performed as Soroopananda, violated Jothi, fathered Karuna, and caused Ellappa to raise her. I too did not yet know it.

"'On learning that Ellappa refused only for want of Jatadharan's consent, I went straight to our village and appealed to him. At first he offered excuses: no divine command had come; the match was unsuitable. I would not leave. At last he seemed to take pity on me.

"'If you want Karuna, obtain the consent of diamond merchant Sokkalingam Chettiar,' said the man dressed as an ascetic. 'Tell him of your love and plead with him. If he agrees, I shall conduct your marriage.'

"'What has Karuna to do with Sokkalingam Chettiar? Why ask one man to consent to another man's daughter's marriage?' I demanded. Only then did Jatadharan tell me the whole story.

"'I was enraged and wanted to eradicate this entire brood of hypocrites who sit in monasteries piling fraud upon fraud. But my heart was fixed upon Karuna. I decided we could destroy the frauds later and should first obtain Chettiar's consent. I met him repeatedly, told him I knew his whole deception, and demanded and warned him to arrange my marriage to Karuna.

"'Chettiar refused to yield. "Jatadharan is a fool who poured the secret out to you. Do not dream that knowing it lets you control me. You cannot even move me! I will not give you Karuna. You are a Christian. How dare you ask me to arrange her marriage to you?" he abused me.

"'I pleaded many times and sometimes threatened him. "It is true that Sokkalingam is Soroopananda and that Karuna was born to Soroopananda and Jothi. But how will you prove those truths?" the rogue asked.

"'I said Jatadharan could testify.

"'"Will Jatadharan, who shared everything with me and now plays the Guru through my help, appear as your witness? He told you something about old events when he was drunk. Go and ask him now to testify."

"'When I did, Jatadharan dismissed me with, "Do you take me for a madman?" Today I asked Sokkalingam, the former Soroopananda, one final time. He said no.

"'That is my story. I need evidence proving somehow that Sokkalingam is Swami Soroopananda. If any of Jatadharan's letters can be found, the drama of these deceivers who ruin our country will be exposed and the people saved.'

"Irudayanathan pleaded for my help. I tried in every possible way, but could not obtain even one letter. Irudayanathan forgot even Karuna in his eagerness to expose the rogues in saffron.

"From this morning onward we went together to many lawyers' houses - rather, he took me along. Every lawyer asked the same questions: 'Nonsense! What is the evidence? Where is the proof? Who is the witness?'

"Some frightened Irudayanathan away. 'He is a prominent merchant. Do you know how grave it is to accuse him of impersonation? You have no proof. You say some Jatadharan told you, then say the man will not testify. What clown would stand in court and argue such a bizarre case? Chettiar will sue you for defamation.'

"Irudayanathan's proposed case was perfectly clear:

"Diamond merchant Sokkalingam Chettiar had, twenty years earlier, disguised himself as an ascetic named Swami Soroopananda and deceived the ignorant people near Tirunelveli to obtain their money. During that time he violated a widow named Jothi, who bore a daughter. When Jothi died, he passed the child off as a divine gift and gave her to the unsuspecting devotee Ellappa Mudaliar to adopt.

"Thus Sokkalingam Chettiar was a hypocrite, libertine, impostor in saffron, and violator of a woman. His property was the result of exploiting the town through religious disguise.

"The drunken Sadaiyan, now called Jatadharan and occupying the Guru's seat, knew the entire fraud, for he had been Sokkalingam's chief disciple and accomplice.

"The compassionate government should investigate the case, compel Jatadharan to speak the truth, expose the fraud of rogues who wander in saffron, and protect poor people from becoming prey to such cheats.

"That was the case Irudayanathan wished to file. All day we climbed the steps of one lawyer's house after another, asking someone to conduct it. Every one laughed and demanded proof, witness, and evidence. One lawyer even cursed him.

"He thundered, 'Great sinner! You malign Soroopananda? I have received his darshan. What do you know of the nature of sages?'

"Sokkalingam, who conducted the fraud, admitted it when speaking to Irudayanathan. Sadaiyan, his accomplice, admitted it too. Yet neither would admit it in court, and the case could not stand. There was evidence that Swami Soroopananda had 'merged into the Divine Light': one of those who preached at the commemorative event was this town's judge. In such a situation, how could Irudayanathan bring a case?

"Searching endlessly for a lawyer inflamed his anger. He took me straight to Karuna's house and poured out the whole story in front of Ellappa Mudaliar.

"'Look, Karuna! You said you would marry such a madman. Look at this lunatic,' Ellappa said.

"Irudayanathan's rage reached his head, and he attacked Ellappa. That was enough. Ellappa's servants gathered and beat him mercilessly. I ran and escaped. Bleeding heavily after the savage blows, Irudayanathan came out of Ellappa's bungalow. I returned, supported him, and brought him here.

"All along the way he kept saying, 'If only I find one good lawyer, I will expose Sokkalingam's entire history to the town and send him to prison for ten years!'

"Compounder, that is the story of my unfortunate friend," Emperuman concluded.

The compounder was stunned.

"Are you wondering whether such frauds can occur in the world?" Emperuman asked.

"No, sir. There are many rogues in saffron. Examine the ancestry of each one's religious banner and you will find some fraud of this kind. As long as people willing to be deceived exist, deceivers will exist too. That is not what stunned me. What Irudayanathan says appears true. What enmity has he with Sokkalingam or Sadaiyan? Poor man! But the court will not accept his truth. There is no evidence for the case. They will dismiss it as a fabrication and may even bring a defamation suit against Irudayanathan. That is what I was thinking," said the compounder.

Dawn came. Irudayanathan opened his eyes, looking exhausted. The doctor examined his pulse and gave him medicine. Within a week he could move about as before, but every former memory had vanished.

Karuna, Ellappa Mudaliar, Sokkalingam, Soroopananda, Sadaiyan - all had disappeared from his mind. He had even forgotten Emperuman. His old self and mind had undergone an extraordinary change. Emperuman tried repeatedly to awaken the old memories, but Irudayanathan could understand nothing. Tears filled Emperuman's eyes.

The doctor declared that the shock of the powerful blow to his head had damaged Irudayanathan's brain. No song, dance, or prank revealed him as a madman. Whenever he saw anyone before him, Irudayanathan asked only, "Are you a lawyer, sir?" He spoke no other words.

Emperuman continued caring for the helpless man. Irudayanathan would eat and go out wandering. When he returned, Emperuman would ask, "Irudayam, where did you go, my boy?"

"To a lawyer's house," Irudayanathan would reply.

Who would ever come forward to act as his lawyer?

*March 1946*

---NOTES---
The two pages previously recorded as having no OCR text were recovered from transparency-bearing PNG scans before translation. The retained Tamil source block is the corrected OCR-stage output and is not silently rewritten.

The title literally says that what Irudayanathan sought was a lawyer; the story repeatedly contrasts this search with his arrival at a doctor's house. “He Sought a Lawyer” preserves that governing phrase.

The source's final date is OCR-damaged as “$-3-46”; the legible month and year are rendered conservatively as March 1946 without inventing a day.
